import <semiconductors> as dev;

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//  1N4007 — 1000 V / 1 A general-purpose mains rectifier (DO-41)
//
//  The other end of the diode world from the 1N4148: slow, rugged, and meant
//  for 50/60 Hz. Card values, the standard PSpice model:
//
//    Is=14.11n  N=1.984   Rs=33.89m  Ikf=94.81  Isr=1.6n  Nr=2
//    Cjo=25.89p M=.44     Vj=.3245   Fc=.5      Bv=1000   Ibv=10u
//    Tt=5.7u
//
//  Deviations: Vt fixed at 0.026 V, and the card's Xti=3 Eg=1.11 temperature
//  scaling is not modelled.
//
//  Tt = 5.7 us is the whole story of this part. It is 285 times the 1N4148's,
//  which means several microseconds of reverse recovery — longer than an
//  entire switching period at 100 kHz. Put one in a buck converter and it
//  conducts backwards through most of every cycle. It is also why the same
//  1 A part that is perfectly happy in a mains bridge is useless as a
//  freewheel diode next to a PWM'd inductor.
//
//  In the other direction Ikf = 94.81 A is effectively no knee at all within
//  its rating, and Rs = 34 mOhm is 20x lower than the 1N4148's — this is a
//  part built to carry current, not to switch.
//
//  The 1N400x series is one die in seven reverse gradings, so this card is the
//  1N4001's with Bv raised from 50 V to 1000 V and nothing else changed. That
//  single number is usually the whole decision: a bridge sees a peak inverse
//  voltage of twice the transformer peak, so a 24 V peak secondary puts 48 V
//  across each blocking diode and a 1N4001 is already in breakdown. See
//  examples/Diode/rectifier.hvr, which fails to converge with the 4001 for
//  exactly that reason — the model conducts in reverse because the real part
//  would.
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

analog module D1N4007<>()[wire anode, wire cathode] {
    module d = dev.Diode<14.11n, 1.984,          // Is, N
                         33.89m,                 // Rs
                         94.81,                  // Ikf
                         1.6n, 2.0,              // Isr, Nr
                         1000.0, 10u,            // Bv, Ibv
                         25.89p, 0.3245, 0.44, 0.5,  // Cjo, Vj, M, Fc
                         5.7u,                   // Tt
                         0.026>()                // Vt
                        [anode, cathode];
}
